{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Dunkirk, Allegheny Valley and Pittsburgh Railroad (DAV&P) ran about 90 miles from the New York Central mainline at Dunkirk, NY (on Lake Erie) south-southwest through Chautauqua County farm towns into the Pennsylvania oil region, terminating at Titusville. It traces back to an 1867-chartered Dunkirk, Warren & Pittsburgh Railroad Company (first passenger train June 22 1871, Dunkirk to at least Falconer), formally becoming the DAV&P via an 1872 merger with the Warren and Venango Railroad. Just four days after that December 1872 incorporation, the whole line was leased to the New York Central and Hudson River Railroad (January 3 1873) for a 501-year term -- effectively immediately, so almost this entire railroad's operating life was under New York Central control. Regular passenger service ended in 1937, with a brief wartime revival. Geometry is real-traced from OpenStreetMap for the Dunkirk-Fredonia-Cassadaga stretch and the North Warren-Warren-Youngsville-Titusville stretch, which resolved as two separate, disconnected fragments of surviving track/right-of-way; the Cassadaga-Falconer, Falconer-Frewsburg, and Frewsburg-North Warren stretches (this obscure, fully-abandoned line has no heritage operation or rail-trail preserving it) had no connected tagging and are straight-line approximations between the real, correctly-ordered station towns.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunkirk,_Allegheny_Valley_and_Pittsburgh_Railroad (Dunkirk, Warren & Pittsburgh Railroad Company chartered by NY Legislature April 1867; construction began June 17 1867; first passenger train June 22 1871, Dunkirk to at least Falconer; Dunkirk, Allegheny and Pittsburgh incorporated Dec 31 1872, merging Dunkirk Warren & Pittsburgh with the Warren and Venango Railroad; leased to New York Central and Hudson River RR for 501 years starting Jan 3 1873; later wholly owned by NYC, then Penn Central; regular passenger service dropped 1937 except for a brief WWII revival)", "search results citing: the original idea dated to c. 1833 among Warren, PA businessmen wanting a Conewango River valley route to Lake Erie; stock first sold 1853; real progress only from winter 1866; the line was 90 miles Dunkirk NY to Titusville PA; full station list Dunkirk-Fredonia-Laona-Lily Dale-Cassadaga-Moons-Sinclairville-Gerry-Rose Mill-Falconer-Frewsburg-Fentonville-Ackley's-Russell-North Warren-Warren-Irvineton-Youngsville-Pittsfield-Garland-Torpedo-Newton-Grand Valley-Selkirk-East Titusville-Titusville"], "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API, overpass.openstreetmap.fr mirror): railway=rail (active) combined with railway=abandoned/disused tagging; routed via Dijkstra shortest path between each station's geocoded coordinates. Two disconnected real-track fragments were found (Dunkirk-Fredonia-Cassadaga, and North Warren-Warren-Youngsville-Titusville); Falconer's own nearby tagging did not connect plausibly to either neighboring fragment (its one candidate path was an implausible ~111km detour vs a 27.6km straight line), so the Cassadaga-Falconer, Falconer-Frewsburg, and Frewsburg-North Warren stretches are straight-line fallbacks."], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for any era; the precise later date the DAV&P became wholly NYC-owned (as opposed to merely leased); details of the brief WWII passenger revival.", "pulled": "20260824", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Dunkirk, Allegheny Valley and Pittsburgh Railroad", "line": "Dunkirk, Allegheny Valley and Pittsburgh: Dunkirk \u2013 Titusville", "id": "dunkirk-allegheny-valley-pittsburgh-railroad", "color": "#a4462a", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated: 1870s rural steam branch line through the New York/Pennsylvania oil region; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1871, "end": 1873, "headway_min": 480, "note": "The idea dated to around 1833 (Warren, PA businessmen wanting a line down the Conewango River valley to Lake Erie), but serious progress only began in the winter of 1866. The New York State Legislature chartered the Dunkirk, Warren & Pittsburgh Railroad Company in April 1867; construction began June 17 1867. The first passenger train ran June 22 1871, from Dunkirk, NY to at least Falconer, NY. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunkirk", "search results citing: the original idea dated to c. 1833 among Warren, PA businessmen wanting a Conewango River valley \u2026"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-79.30069, 42.29758], "to": [-79.19809, 42.11868], "chord_mi": 13.44, "traced_mi": 15.19, "snap_m": [0, 230], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Valley Branch [abandoned] (15.19 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 13 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-79.33456, 42.48322], [-79.34133, 42.48399], [-79.32148, 42.48681], [-79.32247, 42.48636], [-79.32322, 42.4851], [-79.32403, 42.48444], [-79.32436, 42